{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfc5DwxnDi4qzHho2Z2iF", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7553, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7553}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "660cfce4-a580-4854-a2be-004ae9dd140a", "uuid": "7d87851a-e21a-42b8-8be5-b27b29994d77", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T19:31:36.744Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfc5DwbwkQ7AhnyTzNYNw"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfc5DwxnDi4qzHho2Z2iF", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "`git rebase --onto` lets you choose where the replayed commits land separately from which commits get replayed. A plain `git rebase <upstream>` has to use one branch for both jobs.\n\n## Syntax\n\n```\ngit rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> [<branch>]\n```\n\n- **`<upstream>`**: the cutoff. Git takes the commits that are on `<branch>` but not on `<upstream>`, i.e. `upstream..branch`.\n- **`<newbase>`**: where those commits are replayed.\n- **`<branch>`**: the branch being moved. If you leave it out, the current branch is used.\n\nYou can read it as: \"take the commits after `<upstream>` on `<branch>` and put them on `<newbase>`.\"\n\n## Example: move a branch off another feature branch\n\nSay you created `feature-b` from `feature-a`, but `feature-b` doesn't actually need anything from `feature-a`:\n\n```\nmain:       A---B---C\n                     \\\nfeature-a:            D---E\n                           \\\nfeature-b:                  F---G\n```\n\nRunning `git rebase main feature-b` would replay D, E, F and G, because none of those are on `main`. You only want F and G:\n\n```\ngit rebase --onto main feature-a feature-b\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```\nmain:       A---B---C\n                    |\\\nfeature-a:          | D---E\n                     \\\nfeature-b:            F'---G'\n```\n\nF and G are replayed onto `main` as new commits (F', G'), and D and E are left out of `feature-b`.\n\n## Other common uses\n\n- **Delete commits from the middle of a branch.** To drop commits 2 and 3 of `HEAD~3` (keeping only the newest one):\n  ```\n  git rebase --onto HEAD~3 HEAD~1\n  ```\n  This replays only the commits after `HEAD~1` onto `HEAD~3`.\n- **After the parent branch was squash-merged or rewritten.** Pass the old parent commit's SHA as `<upstream>`, so git doesn't try to replay commits that are already in `main` in squashed form:\n  ```\n  git rebase --onto main <old-parent-sha> my-branch\n  ```\n\n## Caveats\n\n- Rebasing rewrites commit hashes. Avoid doing it on branches other people have already pulled.\n- If something goes wrong, `git rebase --abort` stops a rebase that's still in progress. After it finishes, `git reflog` lets you find and restore the original commits."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7553, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7553}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "660cfce4-a580-4854-a2be-004ae9dd140a", "uuid": "6e057fae-8544-4b3d-a800-79f18ea3dcfe", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T19:31:44.045Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfc5DwbwkQ7AhnyTzNYNw"}
